N5 = """About 50 seconds. PROPOSED architecture. Not built.
Say: One brain behind every door. A goal the user states once. One record of applications, deadlines, replies and outcomes, where unknown stays unknown. A loop that speaks when something changes and stays quiet otherwise. A yes collected by Tsenta, bound to the exact action and version, used once. Then act, keep the receipt, and write the outcome back.
Design principle: the job agent stays the core. Tsenta owns the core and each user's career record, not any host's memory, separate from any agent framework or model, so the runtime can change and a closed life experiment never loses the job record. Career-record details (event log, views, provenance, export, delete) are proposed in the runtime review (appendix A7) and are not claimed as built.
Why Tsenta holds the yes: host approval defaults vary and can be loose. ChatGPT's "Allow low-risk actions" applies when nothing overrides it (help.openai.com/en/articles/11487775); Claude's research mode "can invoke tools from your connectors automatically without further approval" (support.claude.com/en/articles/11175166). Per-action prompts are a weak gate: "users approve 97% of permission prompts in Claude Code" (claude.com/blog/auto-mode-default-in-claude-code; coding context, transfer is an inference).
Approval contract (research decision, Oct 14; design, MCP route not shipped): your agent proposes and the call returns a pending id; you confirm on a Tsenta surface; the yes binds the exact payload digest, the user, a short TTL, single use; one execution with a dedupe key; an append-only log. Host prompts are defense in depth, never the control.
Proven: nothing. The prototype screenshots (slide 6) shows the approval behavior on synthetic data only."""

N6 = """About 50 seconds. PROPOSED. Integration across hosts is a goal, not a verified capability.
Say first: both paths meet here. Left, Tsenta's own agent, for anyone who switches or has no agent. Right, the connect path: your agent, through Tsenta's MCP core. Same record, same yes.
Say the approved line verbatim: "Designed to work with MCP-compatible agents; proactive wake where supported, owned-channel fallback elsewhere."
Three parts: (1) one standard MCP core (reads safe to poll; design, not shipped: employer-facing writes marked destructive and routed back to Tsenta for the yes, whichever agent asks); (2) optional per-host wake paths only where a host documents one; (3) Tsenta's own channel as the fallback for hosts without a wake path and users without a host.
Facts (host docs read Oct 13, re-read Oct 14, nothing tested):
- Standard MCP 2026-07-28: "Every interaction begins with the client"; servers must not initiate requests. https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/basic/patterns
- Exception path: ChatGPT MCP Events: "MCP Events lets ChatGPT subscribe to updates from your MCP server"; available in Work chats and with dots; requires protocol 2026-07-28; based on a draft MCP Events specification. ChatGPT decides what the user sees. https://developers.openai.com/plugins/build/mcp-events.md
- ChatGPT event-triggered tasks in Work are documented for Plus and up; whether Tsenta's events qualify, or work on mobile, is unknown.
- Muse: Meta-reviewed connectors, and users can create custom connectors, but Meta's page never mentions MCP, so background use of outside connectors is unknown. Instinct: no MCP, plugin or API route published (unknown, not "no").
- So do not say MCP can never be proactive, and do not say Tsenta works with every agent or is proactive in any AI.
- Claude scheduled tasks and Claude Code routines (1 hour minimum), Gemini schedules (manual confirmation for any write), Cursor automations, Poke inbound API: see appendix A2.
Owned channels each require the user to act first and follow their own rules (WhatsApp 24-hour window and templates; Apple Messages for Business requires live agents)."""

