NAO = """Appendix. CONDITIONAL TRIAL; PRODUCTION NOT SELECTED OR BUILT. From the reconciled build verdict of the runtime review (v3, Oct 13). The demo agent is a real OpenClaw worker, agent loop and local tools on dummy records, tested October 13 and 14. Eight runs so far. Runs 1 to 5 stopped before step 24; runs 6 and 7 passed all 24 machine checks, but a truth review found untrue replies. Run 8, the final run (Oct 14), passed all 24 machine checks, and three reviews found no false statement of record fact. It is a qualified pass, not a clean one: at step 8 it suggested the salary floor as the answer to a seeded injection email's sender (hedged, nothing shared), and the core's resume lookup returns an older version. The others stopped at step 9 (that check was then reworded), at step 12 (a script and engine mismatch, since fixed) and at step 4 (a wording check that read a correct answer as wrong). Effects: the final run made one sandbox submission and one local-outbox email to a fictional .example address; no run sent or submitted anything real. Not run live: the 130-prompt stress set and the security set. The core went offline after the final run. A core-only restart during run 5 kept the session and context (machine and manual check); it is separate from the gate. It is not a production integration and makes no tenancy or isolation claim: the gates on A8 and A9 still decide the runtime, and the SDK baseline is still compared. No real user data, no subscriptions, no Hermes changes.
Pitch line: Tsenta owns the core; the runtime is a swappable worker; we are evaluating OpenClaw as the orchestration worker against a thin-SDK baseline behind the same core.
Core: Tsenta's own, built from scratch (tenancy and identity, credential vault, signed per-action approvals, the portable record with export and delete, scheduler, audit and RBAC, egress, quotas). No candidate provides these for many users.
Conditional trial: OpenClaw as an isolated per-user worker behind the core, one container or OS user per user, sleeping when idle. Fork shape: strip at packaging, with a small patch queue on a pinned stable tag, not deep deletion. OpenClaw's own security policy says it is "not designed as a shared multi-tenant boundary between adversarial users on one gateway"; "Recommended mode: one user per machine/host (or VPS)"; and "If multiple users need OpenClaw, use one VPS (or host/OS user boundary) per user". A container or OS-user boundary per user is within that stated model, which is why each user gets an isolated worker and the core holds tenancy, the record and approvals.
Why OpenClaw leads the trial: the evaluation criteria put orchestration and coding-agent connections in scope, and on those OpenClaw documents the most. Per the OpenClaw docs index (https://docs.openclaw.ai/llms.txt, read Oct 13; index descriptions only, page bodies not read): "openclaw attach" launches Claude Code with a scoped Gateway MCP grant (cli/attach); ACP bindings and Codex app-server lanes (help/testing-live/acp-and-codex); Cloud Workers "Dispatch session work to throwaway cloud machines with OpenClaw worker turns or Codex remote execution" (gateway/cloud-workers); Swarm orchestrates concurrent sub-agents with bounded fan-out and live progress (tools/swarm); a context engine with "pluggable context assembly, compaction, and subagent lifecycle" (concepts/context-engine); cross-agent session and subagent tools (gateway/config-tools/sessions-and-subagents); and a Docker sandbox backend (gateway/sandboxing/docker-backend). Hermes documents less orchestration publicly, and on a thin SDK this layer would be a large, unpriced build. That is why OpenClaw is the trial candidate. It still wins nothing until it passes the gates; for the narrow job flow alone the SDK is the cleaner fit, which is why it stays the baseline.
Caveat, read at source Oct 13: OpenClaw's defaults for delegated coding agents are permissive. Its Codex harness page says local stdio app-server sessions "default to YOLO mode", with approvalPolicy "never" and sandbox "danger-full-access" (https://docs.openclaw.ai/plugins/codex-harness-reference/approval-and-sandbox ; repo copy https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/047b629eb8fcbbda4f9ef4e0f834b57af005ef3d/docs/plugins/codex-harness-reference/approval-and-sandbox.md). Its ACP agents page says "ACPX harness permissions are separate from OpenClaw exec approvals and separate from CLI-backend vendor bypass flags" (https://docs.openclaw.ai/tools/acp-agents-setup). So a Tsenta deployment must override these defaults (gate 11), and the bake-off must test every effective child permission for an explicit deny (gate 7).
Baseline and fallback: a thin model-agnostic SDK, for example Pydantic AI with durable execution on Postgres (DBOS). The runtime review ranks it first, the Vercel AI SDK second (first if the core is TypeScript) and the OpenAI Agents SDK third; the Claude Agent SDK fits only as a delegated coding worker. It becomes the runtime if OpenClaw misses a gate (appendices A8 and A9, 15 gates), with orchestration then built step by step.
Optional challenger: Hermes Agent, single-tenant by its own policy ("a single-tenant personal agent"); it can be benchmarked behind the same core.
Not the starting method: deep source deletion. It is possible, and deleted paths do not turn every upstream change into a conflict (modify and delete conflicts can be resolved mechanically). The real cost is that Tsenta would own manual review of upstream security fixes in deleted code it later needs, and of refactors that move code into kept paths. So start with the packaging strip and treat deeper deletion as a later optimization.
Muse: Meta says it "built Muse from the ground up". TechCrunch (Sep 22, 2026) quotes Nat Friedman: "We built Muse from scratch, but it is definitely heavily inspired as a product by OpenClaw". The sources affirm a from-scratch build and product inspiration; they cannot prove the absence of a fork, so say only that a code fork is not demonstrated. Muse is not evidence that OpenClaw suits Tsenta.
License: OpenClaw, Hermes, Pydantic AI and DBOS are MIT, which permits commercial use with notices kept. That is not dependency clearance: a license scanner and counsel review come before any distribution.
Sources: OpenClaw SECURITY.md https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/047b629eb8fcbbda4f9ef4e0f834b57af005ef3d/SECURITY.md (all three quotes checked verbatim at this commit, Oct 13) ; OpenClaw docs index https://docs.openclaw.ai/llms.txt ; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent/e06df9c2eae6901f4c88f464e0ae6e753c0428b8/SECURITY.md ; https://ai.pydantic.dev/durable_execution/dbos/ ; https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/22/meta-admits-muses-likeness-to-openclaw-isnt-a-coincidence/"""

NAG = """Appendix. PROPOSED GATES, NOTHING BUILT FOR PRODUCTION. From the reconciled build verdict (v3, Oct 13), section 4: 15 conditions before any production commitment to an OpenClaw worker. This slide shows the core gates 1 to 7; enterprise gates 8 to 15 are on appendix A9.
1 Isolation: one container or OS user per end user, sleeping when idle; no shared gateway; cross-user canary at 0 leaks.
2 Packaging strip: only the needed extensions; no ClawHub or other runtime installs; unused channels off (Tsenta's channels stay in the core).
3 Credentials and egress: short-lived tokens from the core vault only; an egress allow-list around the whole worker.
4 Memory: OpenClaw memory and workspace files are caches; context is fed from the Tsenta record, and writes go back as core events with provenance.
5 Side effects: only through Tsenta tools that verify a signed approval bound to the payload hash; OpenClaw's own approvals are a second line only.
6 Security exposure: a pinned stable release with no known unpatched high or critical advisory in kept components, decided by a deduplicated exposure review, not raw counts.
7 Bake-off against the SDK baseline with the same core, model and tasks: approval-gated submit with 0 unapproved side effects; scheduled delivery on time, with tokens and cost at p50 and p95; a multi-day thread across compaction with facts retained; crash and resume with no duplicate side effect; delegate to Claude Code or Codex and resume with approval relayed; a one-version upgrade passing contract tests and evals. It also includes an approval-bypass test: enumerate every effective child permission (the Codex harness approval and sandbox mode, the ACPX permission mode, and CLI vendor bypass flags) and confirm each is explicitly denied, because these layers are separate from OpenClaw exec approvals and local Codex sessions default to YOLO (approvalPolicy "never", sandbox "danger-full-access"); sources in the appendix A7 notes.
OpenClaw is the runtime only if it passes 1 to 6 and 8 to 15 and matches or beats the baseline on 7 at acceptable per-user worker cost. Otherwise the SDK baseline is the runtime, with orchestration built step by step. The same gates apply to whichever runtime is chosen. Per-user worker cost belongs to the cost model; no figure is claimed here."""

NAG2 = """Appendix. PROPOSED ENTERPRISE GATES, NOTHING BUILT FOR PRODUCTION. Gates 8 to 15 of the reconciled build verdict (v3, Oct 13), added from the enterprise cross-check. They apply to whichever runtime is chosen, the SDK baseline included.
8 Lifecycle reach: export and delete reach the worker's workspace, transcripts, caches, telemetry and log copies, and backups.
9 Consent and domain filtering: the context engine filters by consent and domain; a canary life fact never appears in an employer-facing payload.
10 Audit: every worker tool call and approval relay lands in the core audit log with execution identity.
11 Delegated coding agents: delegated Claude Code and Codex sessions are sandboxed, with no Tsenta credentials and no side-effect tools; permissive harness presets are off; a harness approval never substitutes for a Tsenta signature. This overrides OpenClaw's documented default: local stdio Codex sessions "default to YOLO mode" (approvalPolicy "never", sandbox "danger-full-access"), and ACPX harness permissions are separate from OpenClaw exec approvals and from vendor bypass flags such as Claude CLI bypassPermissions (https://docs.openclaw.ai/plugins/codex-harness-reference/approval-and-sandbox ; https://docs.openclaw.ai/tools/acp-agents-setup).
12 Red team: prompt injection through job posts, resumes and emails, as part of the bake-off; pass means 0 unapproved actions and 0 exfiltration.
13 Legal: Gmail API Limited Use compliance inside the worker, model-vendor commercial terms for delegation, and a transitive npm license scan.
14 Operations: a cold-start SLO for sleeping workers, a named image owner, and a tested upgrade and rollback.
15 Cross-user outcome patterns (insights such as 'people like you got interviews'): before any cross-user insight ships, a data-source map separates first-party apply records from Gmail-derived replies; Gmail-derived inputs are excluded until counsel signs off; a minimum cohort size applies before any pattern is shown; and every pattern carries a "correlation, not cause" label, because the data is selection-biased. Google's API Services User Data Policy applies Limited Use to data "aggregated, anonymized, or derived from" Workspace API data, allows transfers only for prominent user-facing features with the user's consent (plus listed exceptions such as security, legal compliance and aggregated internal operations), and prohibits transferring or selling user data to third parties such as data brokers. This is not a blanket ban on every employer-facing use: anything beyond those terms is scoped to counsel review, neither assumed allowed nor assumed banned. Source: https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy (read Oct 13)."""


